  "response": "A username appearing in a log entry conclusively establishes that the account holder performed the recorded action. That principle and the tribunal's ruling that log entries bearing a user's name do not by themselves establish that the user carried out the recorded activity cannot both be true. The investigative question is therefore framed as a strict pair of mutually exclusive possibilities: either the credentials were used by the registered account holder, or the credentials were used by someone other than the registered account holder, and exactly one of the two must hold.\n\nBecause VPN concentrator logs record the originating IP address for each remote session, responders compare the account's login source IP addresses against the employee's known work locations. Impossible-travel analysis flags authentications from geographically distant locations within a short interval. Device fingerprints such as user-agent strings and host identifiers distinguish managed corporate endpoints from unknown machines, and a session traced to an unknown machine indicates that the credentials were in the hands of someone other than the registered account holder. Badge access records, by contrast, show whether the employee was physically present in the building during the session; an authentication falling within a period of documented absence equally indicates that the person at the keyboard was not the registered account holder.\n\nSession cookies cannot be stolen once a multi-factor authentication challenge has been completed. Multi-factor authentication push notification logs, on the other hand, show whether the account holder approved the challenge, which allows responders to ask whether a human being actually consented to each session rather than inferring consent from the mere presence of a successful authentication record.\n\nInvestigators preserve a forensic disk image of the employee's workstation before conducting analysis, a step brought about by the requirement that chain-of-custody documentation be maintained for digital evidence to remain admissible. Only once that image is secured does the analytical work proper begin: timeline reconstruction correlates authentication events with subsequent file access events, so that the volume, sequence and selectivity of the documents touched can be weighed against what the account holder's role would ordinarily require. A deliberate insider exfiltration and an external intruder operating on stolen credentials tend to leave different signatures in that reconstructed sequence, and it is the convergence of device, location, physical-presence and approval evidence, rather than any single artefact, that allows responders to attribute the activity with confidence.",
